<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heinkel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ernst Heinkel Flugzeugwerke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heinkel was a German aircraft maker founded by Ernst Heinkel at Warnemünde in 1922 and based in Rostock. It built the He 111 bomber, a mainstay of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and the He 178 of 1939, the first aircraft to fly on a turbojet engine. Its Rostock works were heavily bombed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ernst Heinkel had been chief designer for the Hansa-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> aviation firm in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. He founded his own company at Warnemünde in 1922, after the restrictions on German aviation imposed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> were relaxed. By 1929 its compressed-air <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a> were launching short-range mail planes from the decks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norddeutscher-lloyd">Norddeutscher Lloyd</a> liners <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and Europa.</p><p>Its first major aircraft success after the war was the all-metal, single-engined He 70 Blitz, a high-speed mail plane and airliner built for Deutsche Luft Hansa in 1932, which broke a number of speed records for its class. The twin-engined He 111 Doppel-Blitz followed. Among the firm's chief designers were the twin brothers Siegfried and Walter Günter and Heinrich Hertel. Its headquarters, later called Heinkel-Nord, stood in Rostock, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a> of 1938 a second works, Heinkel-Süd, was set up at Schwechat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the He 70 and above all the He 111 were adapted as bombers for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a>. Heinkel also built the He 177 Greif, the Luftwaffe's only heavy bomber to enter service, which was never deployed in large numbers and was troubled by engine fires. The firm was less successful with fighters: the He 112 lost to the Messerschmitt Bf 109, and the He 100 failed through political interference at the Reich Aviation Ministry. The He 219 night fighter was the first Luftwaffe front-line aircraft with retractable tricycle undercarriage and the first operational military aircraft to use ejection seats, but was built in limited numbers. From 1941 the company was merged with the engine maker Hirth as Heinkel-Hirth.</p><p>In 1939 the He 176 and He 178, flown by Erich Warsitz, became the first aircraft to fly under liquid-fuel rocket and turbojet power. Heinkel was the first to bring a jet fighter to prototype stage, the He 280, but the ministry favoured the rival Messerschmitt Me 262. In early 1942 RAF photographic interpreters at Medmenham saw the He 280 in reconnaissance photographs taken after a raid on the Rostock factory. Very late in the war the single-jet He 162 Spatz flew and barely entered service. Heinkel used between 6,000 and 8,000 prisoners from Sachsenhausen concentration camp to build the He 177.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the war Heinkel was forbidden to build aircraft and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycles</a>, motor scooters, the Perle moped and the Kabine microcar, introduced in 1956. It returned to aircraft in the mid-1950s, building Lockheed F-104 Starfighters under licence for the West German Luftwaffe. In 1965 it was absorbed by Vereinigte Flugtechnische Werke, which Messerschmitt-Bölkow-Blohm took over in 1980; the line later became part of Airbus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heinkel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Test pilot Erich Warsitz flew the <b>Heinkel</b> He 178 at Rostock-Marienehe, the first flight of an aircraft powered by a turbojet.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-27">27 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Rostock's <b>Heinkel</b> and Arado aircraft works made it a target of Allied bombing.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-mecklenburg">Free State of Mecklenburg</a></li></ul></section>
